---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="MyGameDNA is a web application that classifies games across three skill-based dimensions — Micro, Mystiko, and Macro — within separate Challenge and Reward profiles."
  canonicalPath="/about"
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <h1
        class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-(--color-text) sm:text-4xl"
      >
        About
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-10">
      <!-- Section 1: Why was the project created? -->
      <section>
        <h2
          class="mb-4 text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-(--color-text) sm:text-2xl"
        >
          Why was the project created?
        </h2>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
          <p>
            I’ll be completely honest. I have a bit of a problem. I spend too
            much money buying games on Steam only to play it for an hour or two,
            stop playing, and never touch it again. I could get a refund if I
            asked for one immediately as I put the controller (or mouse) down,
            but often, I tend to “give the game another chance,” which could
            take months or years. Even then, on my second playthrough, there are
            just games that I cannot crack past 3 hours no matter how much I
            try. I take the L for that one, move on, accepting the wasted 50
            dollars or so. However, as games continue to get more and more
            expensive (which is a problem by itself, but that is another topic,
            I digress) I start to reevaluate whether this gaming lifestyle is
            sustainable. Spoiler alert: It is not.
          </p>
          <p>One day, I happened upon this brilliant video on YouTube:</p>

          <!-- Compact Inline YouTube Player -->
          <div
            class="my-2 max-w-md overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-(--color-border) bg-(--color-surface) shadow-sm"
          >
            <div class="aspect-video w-full">
              <iframe
                class="h-full w-full"
                src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/NgHvdCcmQ4o?autoplay=0"
                title="Surnex - The Challenge vs Reward Video"
                allow="accelerometer; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
                allowfullscreen
                loading="lazy"></iframe>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            and came to agree mostly with everything in the video, and
            admittedly, it inspired most of this project. As I spent weeks and
            months constantly thinking about this topic, and the model Surnex
            proposed, I figured, maybe there is another layer to it that he
            missed. After all, if games were ONLY about challenge, then… why do
            cheaters exist? I do get that some exist merely to seek joy from
            causing others to suffer (Schadenfreude, if you will), but there
            must be more to it than that. Some people genuinely repent from
            cheating, and when asked why they did it in the first place, when
            the “challenge” is part of the fun of games, that is when the answer
            that most of them give becomes insightful: “It was because I wanted
            ___.” Whatever it was, maybe elo, a skin, or rare items, the common
            trait here is that these cheaters wanted the REWARD at all costs,
            even if they have to forsake the competitive integrity of the game
            and throw the “Challenge” part of the game out of the window
            entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            Thus, I improved upon Surnex’s model by introducing two layers: The
            REWARD and CHALLENGE layers. The goal of the project, in essence,
            was for me to have a method of better classifying games into buckets
            of games that “made me enjoy them” in the same way, through the
            REWARD and CHALLENGE layers, rather than superficial, useless
            generic tags like “JRPG” or “FPS”.
          </p>
          <p>
            Notice, how, even if my definitions of Micro, Macro and Mystiko are
            “completely wrong”, as long as all of them are consistent, games get
            put into buckets that all “felt the same way” in terms of Reward and
            Challenge, so, in a way, it achieves what I wanted even without the
            definitions being “correct” according to some universal truth:
          </p>
          <p class="font-medium text-(--color-text)">
            I can get recommended games that I may actually enjoy playing based
            on the games that I am already playing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Built on Astro, Powered by Django -->
      <section>
        <div class="mb-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
          <h2
            class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-(--color-text) sm:text-2xl"
          >
            Built on Astro, Powered by Django
          </h2>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <img
              src="/images/about/astro-logo.png"
              alt="Astro Logo"
              class="h-6 w-auto object-contain brightness-110"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <img
              src="/images/about/django-logo.png"
              alt="Django Logo"
              class="h-6 w-auto object-contain brightness-110"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
          <p>
            The blazing fasts speeds of the Astro with the robust stability and
            security of Django, this unique tech-stack has (to my knowledge),
            ever been used a handful of times. I initially thought of naming it
            Astro-on-the-Django due to how it sounds, but after I found that
            this stack has been explored before, under the name Djazztro&nbsp;(<a
              href="https://github.com/Bwc9876/Djazztro"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="text-(--color-blue) underline hover:opacity-80"
              >https://github.com/Bwc9876/Djazztro</a
            >),&nbsp;I figured this name is better, as coincidentally, Django
            was also named after a legendary Jazz guitarist.
          </p>
          <p>
            Why Astro, and not React? Well, because I hated React, and wanted to
            do very little of it, if possible. Not a fan of Angular either, but
            I figured, at the end of the day, it’s all javasc.. no, “type”script
            anyway, so who cares. I could be using t3, but it wouldn’t make this
            website any better, plus, the stack wouldn’t have a cool name.
          </p>
          <p>
            Why not Django Templates? Because I wanted really pretty graphs, and
            d3 just works so well with Astro right out of the box. Also, I
            figured decoupling the frontend meant if I ever needed to outsource
            development, it’d be cheaper to hire a TS dev than fullstack Python
            dev (I think?)
          </p>
          <p>
            Why Django? Not Node? Well, truth be told, I don’t very much like
            Javascript nor Typescript, at all. There’s nothing wrong with it (so
            JS devs don’t kill me), I just don’t like it. As for any other
            backend, I did start out with C#, but I’m quite deep into python,
            for data science stuff, and figured, why not just use Django.
          </p>
          <p>Best decision ever. Batteries included frameworks are so nice.</p>
          <p>
            Unhappy with my decisions? The code is public. Just fork it, rewrite
            it in rust, or whatever. I really don’t care. If I needed to use PHP
            for this project, you can bet I’d be learning Laravel right about
            now.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: How YOU can contribute -->
      <section>
        <h2
          class="mb-4 text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-(--color-text) sm:text-2xl"
        >
          How YOU can contribute
        </h2>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
          <p>
            Just keep submitting scores. More data makes the classifications and
            recommendations more accurate.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want to financially support me, you can buy me a coffee
            at:&nbsp;<a
              href="#"
              class="text-(--color-blue) underline hover:opacity-80"
              >(Placeholder Link)</a
            >
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
